0","source_locator":"Main PDF p.6, Experimental, hydrothermal preparations","caption":"NanoXtalSyn-authored conceptual operation illustration; no measured dimensions or unreported conditions."}]},"general_background":[{"id":"hydrothermal","title":"Solvent and a closed reaction environment","summary":"A heated closed solvent environment changes solubility, transport and precursor conversion compared with an open vessel. Changes in solvent properties and dissolution followed by reprecipitation can alter the pathways available for nucleation and crystal growth.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1016/j.pcrysgrow.2007.04.001","url":"https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pcrysgrow.2007.04.001","kind":"review","title":"Hydrothermal technology for nanotechnology","container":"Progress in Crystal Growth and Characterization of Materials","year":2007}},{"id":"amine-ligand","title":"Amine coordination","summary":"An amine nitrogen can donate electron density to suitable solution species or surface sites. Donation from the nitrogen lone pair and competition with other ligands can change precursor speciation and surface association.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr900137k","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr900137k","kind":"review","title":"Prospects of Colloidal Nanocrystals for Electronic and Optoelectronic Applications","container":"Chemical Reviews","year":2009}},{"id":"metal-source","title":"From a metal precursor to reactive species","summary":"A metal precursor supplies the metal, while its ligands and conversion chemistry influence how reactive species become available. Stronger or more persistent coordination can slow release of reactive metal species, while ligand exchange can open alternative conversion pathways.","label":"General background, not the authors' interpretation","reference":{"doi":"10.1021/cr400544s","url":"https://doi.org/10.1021/cr400544s","kind":"review",